### What is this preparation strategy?

Instead of memorizing a separate answer for every possible question, build a small portfolio of deep stories and map them to multiple question types. Almost any behavioral question will map to one of your prepared stories.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;
public class StoryBank {
    record Story(String title, Set<String> categories) {}
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Story> bank = List.of(
            new Story("Transfer service redesign", Set.of("leadership", "tough-decision", "conflict", "technical")),
            new Story("Production outage fix",    Set.of("pressure", "debugging", "communication", "prevention")),
            new Story("Mentoring junior on BigDecimal", Set.of("mentoring", "teaching", "quality")),
            new Story("Migration monolith to service", Set.of("leadership", "risk", "technical", "planning")),
            new Story("Missed deadline recovery", Set.of("failure", "communication", "estimation", "accountability")),
            new Story("Learning Kafka under deadline", Set.of("learning", "adaptability", "delivery")),
            new Story("Saying no to skip fraud check", Set.of("pushback", "judgment", "stakeholder"))
        );
        bank.forEach(s -> System.out.println(s.title() + " -> " + s.categories()));
    }
}
```

### How do you tag stories to categories?

For each story, list the behavioral categories it legitimately covers. A single story that covers leadership, conflict, and a technical decision is worth more than three narrow ones.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class StoryTagging {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Story: Transfer service redesign");
        System.out.println("  Tags: leadership, tough-decision, conflict, technical-depth");
        System.out.println("  For 'leadership' question: emphasize scope and team coordination");
        System.out.println("  For 'conflict' question: emphasize the disagreement with teammate");
        System.out.println("  For 'technical' question: emphasize the sync vs async trade-off");
        System.out.println("Same story, different emphasis.");
    }
}
```

### How do you match a question to a story?

When the question comes, identify the category, look up the best-matching story, and tell it with the emphasis that category wants.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class QuestionMatching {
    public static String match(String questionCategory) {
        return switch (questionCategory) {
            case "leadership" -> "Transfer service redesign (emphasize scope and team)"
            ; case "conflict" -> "Transfer service redesign (emphasize disagreement)"
            ; case "failure" -> "Missed deadline recovery (emphasize accountability)"
            ; case "learning" -> "Kafka ramp-up (emphasize method and speed)"
            ; default -> "Pick the story with the closest tag match"
            ;
        };
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(match("leadership"));
        System.out.println(match("failure"));
    }
}
```

### How do you ensure variety?

Keep a mental checklist: once you have told a story in this interview, do not repeat it. With 5-7 stories, you have enough for any interview without recycling.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;
public class VarietyCheck {
    private static final Set<String> usedStories = new HashSet<>();
    public static boolean canUse(String story) {
        if (usedStories.contains(story)) return false;
        usedStories.add(story);
        return true;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(canUse("Transfer redesign")); // true
        System.out.println(canUse("Transfer redesign")); // false (already used)
        System.out.println(canUse("Outage fix"));         // true
    }
}
```

### Why rehearse two lengths?

Some interviewers want a 2-minute summary. Others want a 5-minute deep-dive. Having both ready means you can match the depth they want.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class TwoLengths {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("2-minute: headline STAR with one-sentence per element");
        System.out.println("5-minute: full STAR with technical detail, numbers, and reflection");
        System.out.println("Start short; expand if the interviewer pulls on the thread.");
    }
}
```

### How do you choose which stories to prepare?

Pick stories that are deep (real challenge, decision, personal action, measurable result) and that together cover all the major categories: leadership, conflict, failure, ambiguity, influence, technical depth, learning, and mentoring.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
import java.util.List;
public class StorySelection {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> requiredCategories = List.of(
            "leadership", "conflict", "failure", "ambiguity",
            "influence", "technical", "learning", "mentoring"
        );
        System.out.println("Your 5-7 stories should collectively cover:");
        requiredCategories.forEach(c -> System.out.println("  - " + c));
    }
}
```

### What is the key insight?

Depth beats breadth. A handful of deeply prepared stories, each reusable across multiple question types, gives far better interview coverage than a long list of shallow ones.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class DepthOverBreadth {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("5 deep stories x 4 tags each = 20 question types covered");
        System.out.println("20 shallow stories x 1 tag each = same coverage but worse delivery");
        System.out.println("Always choose depth.");
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class PrepFollowUps {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Can you give another example? -> Pull a different story from the bank");
        System.out.println("Tell me more about that -> Switch to the 5-minute version");
        System.out.println("Different angle? -> Re-tell same story emphasizing the new angle");
    }
}
```
